      item-39 at level 3: text: Most competitive neural sequence ... ut sequence of symbol representations 
      item-40 at level 3: formula: (x_1, ..., x_n)
      item-41 at level 3: text:  to a sequence of continuous representations 
      item-42 at level 3: formula: \mathbf{z} = (z_1, ..., z_n)
      item-43 at level 3: text: . Given 
      item-44 at level 3: formula: \mathbf{z}
      item-45 at level 3: text: , the decoder then generates an output sequence 
      item-46 at level 3: formula: (y_1,...,y_m)
      item-47 at level 3: text:  of symbols one element at a tim ... tional input when generating the next.
  item-48 at level 1: text: The Transformer follows this ove ...  Figure[fig:model-arch], respectively.
  item-49 at level 1: section_header: Encoder and Decoder Stacks
  item-50 at level 1: text: 
    item-51 at level 2: inline: group group
      item-52 at level 3: text: Encoder:The encoder is composed of a stack of 
      item-53 at level 3: formula: N=6
      item-54 at level 3: text:  identical layers. Each layer ha ... t is, the output of each sub-layer is 
      item-55 at level 3: formula: \mathrm{LayerNorm}(x + \mathrm{Sublayer}(x))
      item-56 at level 3: text: , where 
      item-57 at level 3: formula: \mathrm{Sublayer}(x)
      item-58 at level 3: text:  is the function implemented by  ...  layers, produce outputs of dimension 
      item-59 at level 3: formula: d_{\text{model}}=512
      item-60 at level 3: text: .
  item-61 at level 1: text: 
    item-62 at level 2: inline: group group
      item-63 at level 3: text: Decoder:The decoder is also composed of a stack of 
      item-64 at level 3: formula: N=6
      item-65 at level 3: text:  identical layers.  In addition  ... res that the predictions for position 
      item-66 at level 3: formula: i
      item-67 at level 3: text:  can depend only on the known outputs at positions less than 
      item-68 at level 3: formula: i
      item-69 at level 3: text: .
  item-70 at level 1: section_header: Attention
  item-71 at level 1: text: An attention function can be des ...  the query with the corresponding key.
  item-72 at level 1: section_header: Scaled Dot-Product Attention
  item-73 at level 1: text: 
    item-74 at level 2: inline: group group
      item-75 at level 3: text: We call our particular attention ... ists of queries and keys of dimension 
      item-76 at level 3: formula: d_k
      item-77 at level 3: text: , and values of dimension 
      item-78 at level 3: formula: d_v
      item-79 at level 3: text: .  We compute the dot products of the query with all keys, divide each by 
      item-80 at level 3: formula: \sqrt{d_k}
      item-81 at level 3: text: , and apply a softmax function to obtain the weights on the values.
  item-82 at level 1: text: 
    item-83 at level 2: inline: group group
      item-84 at level 3: text: In practice, we compute the atte ... eously, packed together into a matrix 
      item-85 at level 3: formula: Q
      item-86 at level 3: text: .   The keys and values are also packed together into matrices 
      item-87 at level 3: formula: K
      item-88 at level 3: text:  and 
      item-89 at level 3: formula: V
      item-90 at level 3: text: .  We compute the matrix of outputs as:
  item-91 at level 1: formula: \begin{equation}
   \mathrm{Atte ... rac{QK^T}{\sqrt{d_k}})V
\end{equation}
  item-92 at level 1: text: 
    item-93 at level 2: inline: group group
      item-94 at level 3: text: The two most commonly used atten ... thm, except for the scaling factor of 
      item-95 at level 3: formula: \frac{1}{\sqrt{d_k}}
      item-96 at level 3: text: . Additive attention computes th ...  optimized matrix multiplication code.
  item-97 at level 1: text: 
    item-98 at level 2: inline: group group
      item-99 at level 3: text: While for small values of 
      item-100 at level 3: formula: d_k
      item-101 at level 3: text:  the two mechanisms perform simi ...  without scaling for larger values of 
      item-102 at level 3: formula: d_k
      item-103 at level 3: text:  [DBLP:journals/corr/BritzGLL17]. We suspect that for large values of 
      item-104 at level 3: formula: d_k
      item-105 at level 3: text: , the dot products grow large in ... where it has extremely small gradients
  item-106 at level 1: footnote: 
    item-107 at level 2: inline: group group
      item-108 at level 3: text: To illustrate why the dot produc ...  large, assume that the components of 
      item-109 at level 3: formula: q
      item-110 at level 3: text:  and 
      item-111 at level 3: formula: k
      item-112 at level 3: text:  are independent random variables with mean 
      item-113 at level 3: formula: 0
      item-114 at level 3: text:  and variance 
      item-115 at level 3: formula: 1
      item-116 at level 3: text: .  Then their dot product, 
      item-117 at level 3: formula: q \cdot k = \sum_{i=1}^{d_k} q_ik_i
      item-118 at level 3: text: , has mean 
      item-119 at level 3: formula: 0
      item-120 at level 3: text:  and variance 
      item-121 at level 3: formula: d_k
      item-122 at level 3: text: .
  item-123 at level 1: text: 
    item-124 at level 2: inline: group group
      item-125 at level 3: text: . To counteract this effect, we scale the dot products by 
      item-126 at level 3: formula: \frac{1}{\sqrt{d_k}}
      item-127 at level 3: text: .
  item-128 at level 1: section_header: Multi-Head Attention
  item-129 at level 1: section: group figure
    item-130 at level 2: text: [t]0.5
